Bima now has the project and both sites. In this tutorial, Bima copies the two
collecting events, which bring the rest of the team with them, and the taxa he
needs.

## 1. Copy the events as files

An event carries more than a site: dates, methods, notes, environmental data,
its site, and every person on the event. Here, that is too much for one QR code,
so NAHPU offers to export the event instead.

{% devicepair
   tablet="app-screenshots/tutorials/teamwork/ipad-event-qr-too-large.webp"
   tabletAlt="NAHPU Events page on an iPad for TJHH-P1-2014-09-12 with the dialog: QR code is too large. This record contains too much data for a QR code. Export it as JSON to transfer the complete record instead."
   tabletLabel="Heru · iPad" /%}

{% steps %}

1. On the iPad, Heru opens the `TJHH-L1` event and selects `Export event` from
   the three-dot menu (or `Export event` in the `QR code is too large` dialog).
2. Heru selects `Export`, then `Share`, and sends the JSON file to Bima—for
   example with AirDrop, Nearby Share, a messaging app, or a cable.
3. On the iPhone, Bima opens `Events`, selects `Import event`, and picks the
   file. Keep `Create new event`.
4. In `Choose event site`, select the existing `TJHH-L1`, then `Continue`.
5. Repeat for the `TJHH-P1` event, choosing `TJHH-P1` as its site.

{% /steps %}

{% devicepair
   phone="app-screenshots/tutorials/teamwork/iphone-event-site.webp"
   phoneAlt="NAHPU Choose event site dialog on an iPhone with Create embedded site, TJHH-L1 selected, and TJHH-P1"
   phoneLabel="Bima · iPhone" /%}

`Create embedded site` would add a second copy of the site the event file
carries. Linking the event to the site already scanned keeps one site catalog.
Check that the Event IDs on the iPhone read `TJHH-L1-2014-09-12` and
`TJHH-P1-2014-09-12`, exactly as on the iPad.

The event files also bring Bima Sampel and Citra Contoh to the iPhone. Bima's
record arrives with the `Cataloger` role, initials `BS`, and cataloger number
`1`, so his first specimen becomes `BS1`.

## 2. Copy the taxa Bima needs

Bima catalogs the shrews from Line 1 and a *Maxomys* from his own Line 2, so his
iPhone needs *Crocidura neglecta*, *Crocidura beccarii*, and *Maxomys
hylomyoides*.

{% steps %}

1. On the iPad, Heru opens `Taxon registry`, opens *Crocidura neglecta*, and
   taps its QR code.
2. On the iPhone, Bima opens `Taxon registry` > `Add taxon`, switches to
   `Import`, selects `Scan QR`, and saves the taxon.
3. Repeat for *Crocidura beccarii* and *Maxomys hylomyoides*.

{% /steps %}

Taxa are matched by name when catalogs are merged, so typing the names by hand
also works—as long as the spelling matches exactly.

